-- title:
--   Discreteness and localisation of valuation subrings containing the j-chart algebra
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q\ge 5$ and $\ell\ge 3$ are primes with $\ell\neq q$, and $M'$ is a non-zero natural number divisible by neither $q$ nor $\ell$. The field $L$ has characteristic $0$, $\xi\in L$ is a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity, and the hypothesis `hι` requires the existence of a ring homomorphism $\iota\colon L\to\mathbb C$ with $\iota\xi=\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$. The field $K$ is an intermediate field of $L\subseteq L((Q))$, and `hK` pins it down as [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) applied to the rational $q$-expansion function field [`ModularCurve.xHFunctionField ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH (q*ℓ) M')`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L79), i.e. the $L$-subfield of $L((Q))$ generated by the coefficientwise images of that field; here `levelH (q*ℓ) M'` is the kernel of the reduction map $(\mathbb Z/(q\ell)^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/q\ell)^\times$. The ring $A$ is a henselian discrete valuation domain with algebraically closed residue field, equipped with $A$-algebra structures on $L$ and on $K$ forming a scalar tower, with $L$ its fraction field; `hAq` requires $q\in\mathfrak m_A$ and `hξA` that $\xi$ lies in the image of $A$. The element $j\in K$ is non-zero and, by `hj`, has Laurent expansion the coefficient embedding of the rational series [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) $=Q^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{jNumQ}$. Further, $\varpi$ generates $\mathfrak m_A$, and `hϖt` requires a unit $u\in A$ with $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1}=qu$. Write $C:=$ `chartAlgFin A ↥K j` for the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ consisting of the elements integral over $A[j]$, and $\mathfrak X:=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A ↥K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) for the pushout of $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the two chart algebras along the middle chart, with its structure morphism `toBase` to $\operatorname{Spec}A$.
--
--   The ideal $y\subseteq C$ is maximal (`hy`) and contains the image of $\varpi$ (`hϖy`), and `hss` requires it to be supersingular: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi\colon C\to\Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), the set of $j$-invariants such that every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no non-zero point killed by $q$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hArig` supplies a Drinfeld chart at every supersingular point of the model, and is summarised here: for every point $z$ of $\mathfrak X$ at which the germ $\varpi_z$ of the function coming from $\varpi$ lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk, and every point $y'$ of `XFin` $=\operatorname{Spec}C$ with `ιFin`-image $z$ whose ideal satisfies the supersingularity condition of `hss`, there exist a complete discrete valuation domain $W$, a ring homomorphism $\sigma\colon A\to W$ with $\mathfrak m_W=(\sigma\varpi)$, series $f,u,v\in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u,v$ units and $f\equiv X_0X_1^q-X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and an isomorphism $e$ of the adic completion of the stalk $\mathcal O_{\mathfrak X,z}$ with $S:=W[[X_0,X_1]]/\bigl(C(\sigma(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))v-fu\bigr)$, subject to seven clauses: $e$ sends the image of $a\in A$ to the constant $\sigma a$; every $L$-automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ satisfying `IsLevelAutAt` for $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ preserves $C$ (the predicate `IsLevelAutAt L (q*ℓ) ξ (q*ℓ) ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (levelH (q*ℓ) M') γ⁻¹ K τ` says that $\tau$ acts on quotients of modular forms of level $\Gamma_H((q\ell)^2M')$ with integral $q$-expansions by the substitution $f\mapsto f\mid_k\mathrm{conjElemN}\,(q\ell)\,\gamma^{-1}$, read through any $\iota$ as in `hι`); such $\tau$ with $\gamma$ also in $\Gamma(\ell)$ induce the identity on $C/y'$; for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ inducing the identity modulo $y'$ there are an automorphism $\theta$ of $S$ compatible with $\tau$ through $e$ and the germ map, fixing constants, with $\theta(X_{j})\equiv\sum_i M_{ij}X_i$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by the images of $X_0,X_1$, where $c\in W$ and $M\in\mathrm{Mat}_2(W)$ satisfy $c^{q+1}\equiv 1$ and $M\equiv c\gamma$ modulo $\mathfrak m_W$, with $c\equiv 1$ when $\gamma\in\Gamma(\ell)$ and $c\not\equiv1$ when $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ and $\tau\neq\mathrm{id}$; a separation clause, stating that two primes of $S$ containing the constant $\sigma\varpi$, not containing both images of $X_0,X_1$, and containing elements $a_iX_0+b_iX_1+h_i$ with $h_i\in(X_0,X_1)^2$, have distinct contractions to the stalk whenever $q\nmid a_1b_2-a_2b_1$; a branch clause, identifying, for a prime $P$ of $S$ containing $\sigma\varpi$ and an element $X_0+h$ with $h\in(X_0,X_1)^2$ and not both images of $X_0,X_1$, membership of the germ of $a\in C$ in the contraction of $P$ with the condition that all Laurent coefficients of $a$ lie in $\mathfrak m_A$; and a Hasse-type clause for [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L (q*ℓ)`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18), asserting that it lies in $K$ and in $C$, that it is congruent to some $a_0\in A$ modulo $y'$, and that the image of that difference under $e$ is the class of a series $h\in(X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$, $e_0\ge1$, whose leading form $\sum_{i\le e_0}\mathrm{coeff}_{(i,e_0-i)}(h)\,a^ib^{e_0-i}$ is a unit for all $a,b\in W$ not both in $\mathfrak m_W$ with $a^qb-ab^q\in\mathfrak m_W$.
--
--   Next, a point $z$ of $\mathfrak X$ is fixed together with the germ $\varpi_z$ of $\varpi$ (`hϖz`) lying in the maximal ideal of the stalk (`hz`), a point $y'$ of `XFin` with `ιFin`-image $z$ (`hy'`) satisfying the supersingularity condition `hss'`, and `hy'y`: the ideal of $y'$ equals $y$. A chart witness at $z$ is fixed explicitly: a complete discrete valuation domain $W_1$, a homomorphism $\sigma_1\colon A\to W_1$ with $\mathfrak m_{W_1}=(\sigma_1\varpi)$, series $f_1,u_1,v_1$ with $u_1,v_1$ units and $f_1$ congruent to $X_0X_1^q-X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and an isomorphism $e_1$ of the completed stalk at $z$ with $S_1:=W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/\bigl(C(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))v_1-f_1u_1\bigr)$; the hypothesis `hW₁` requires of this witness exactly the seven clauses listed above, at this $z$ and $y'$.
--
--   The ideal $J\subseteq C$ is, by `hJ`, the infimum of the ideals obtained as follows: for $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$, take the contraction along the restriction of $\tau$ to $C$ of the contraction, along $e_1$ composed with the completion and germ maps, of the ideal of $S_1$ generated by the classes of $C(\sigma_1\varpi_t)$, $X_0$ and $X_1$. The $A$-subalgebra $B\subseteq K$ is, by `hB`, generated over $C$ by $\{x\in K:\ x\varpi_t\in J\}$. The valuation subring $W\subseteq K$ satisfies `hBW`: $B\subseteq W$. Four further blocks are imposed. `hR1`: $C\subseteq B$, and every element of $K$ is a quotient of two elements of $B$ with non-zero denominator. `hR2`: $B$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A$, and $B/\varpi B$ has Krull dimension at most $1$. `hR3` (five clauses): the elements of $L$ lying in $W$ are exactly those of $A$; $\mathfrak m_W$ is generated by the image of $\varpi$; $W$ is a discrete valuation ring; for $b\in C$, $b\in y$ if and only if $b\in\mathfrak m_W$; and $f\in K$ lies in $W$ exactly when $f=g/h$ with $g,h\in B$ and $h\notin\mathfrak m_W$. `hEQ` (five clauses, summarised here): for every $\mathbb F_{q^2}$-algebra structure on the residue field $k$ of $A$ there is a surjection $\rho\colon B\to$ [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q k`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) whose kernel consists of the elements of $\mathfrak m_W$, compatible with $A\to k$, and equivariant for level automorphisms in the sense that for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $W$ there is $c\in\mathbb F_{q^2}^\times$ with $(\gamma\bmod q,c)$ in [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) (the kernel of the character $(g,c)\mapsto\det(g)\,c^{q+1}$) such that $\rho\circ\tau$ is `hAction` of that pair applied to $\rho$, with $c\neq1$ when $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ and $\tau\neq\mathrm{id}$; level automorphisms for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ preserve $B$; every prime of $B$ containing $\varpi$ receives, under some level automorphism with $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, all elements of $B$ lying in $\mathfrak m_W$; an element of $B$ carried into $\mathfrak m_W$ by all such level automorphisms is divisible by $\varpi$ in $B$; and a level automorphism for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ which preserves $y$ preserves $W$.
--
--   The conclusion concerns an arbitrary valuation subring of $K$; the data $W$, $B$, $J$ and the chart witness enter only as hypotheses. It asserts: for every valuation subring $V$ of $K$ such that (i) the elements of $L$ whose images lie in $V$ are exactly those coming from $A$, (ii) the image of $\varpi$ lies in $V$ and belongs to the maximal ideal of $V$, (iii) every element of $C$ lies in $V$, and (iv) there exists $c\in C$ such that for every monic $p\in A[T]$ the value at $c$ of the polynomial obtained by mapping the coefficients of $p$ into $K$ lies in $V$ and outside the maximal ideal of $V$, the following two statements hold: first, $V$ is a discrete valuation ring; second, for every $f\in K$ one has $f\in V$ if and only if there are $g,h\in C$ with the image of $h$ in $K$ lying in $V$ and a unit there, and $f\cdot h=g$ in $K$. Thus $V$ is the localisation of the $j$-chart algebra $C$ at the complement of its centre.
--
--   This is the identification of an Igusa-type valuation ring of the rigid-level function field $K$: under the Drinfeld-chart and equivariance hypotheses at a supersingular point, any valuation subring of $K$ restricting to $A$ on $L$, containing the $j$-chart algebra $C$, with $\varpi$ in its centre and with a chart element whose reduction is generic, is discrete and is the localisation of $C$ at its centre. It is used further on in the analysis of the blown-up supersingular chart and in the proof that level automorphisms coming from $\Gamma(q)$ preserve the Igusa valuation.
